*** Rate Limiter ***


Question is how can we design a rate limiter ? 

Requirements:

Limit requests based on client identifier (IP, API key, user ID)
Support different limits for different endpoints or user tiers
Minimal latency impact
Distributed system compatibility
Scalability to handle high traffic

> Token Bucket: we have a bucket which holds fixed number of token , initially bucket has x tokens , when each request comes it consumes a token , if bucket is empty that request is rejected . We have a refilling service also which fills token back in bucket at some rate

> Leaky Bucket: Models a bucket with a constant outflow rate and a maximum capacity. Requests are added to the bucket. Bucket "leaks" at a constant rate.
If bucket is full, new requests are rejected.

> Fixed window counter :: allows fixed number of requests in a time window, resets counter in each new window 

> SLiding window counter :: Assign a timestamp to each request if another request is sent while the previous request is still in window we dont allow that 

Assume a limit of 10 requests per minute:

Fixed Window:

Minute 1: 0:00 - 0:59, 10 requests allowed
Minute 2: 1:00 - 1:59, 10 requests allowed
A user could potentially send 10 requests at 0:59 and another 10 at 1:01, getting 20 requests through in 2 seconds.

Leaky Bucket:

Constant outflow of 1 request per 6 seconds
Even if 20 requests arrive in 2 seconds, only 2 would be processed, and the rest would be either queued (if space in bucket) or rejected


Implementation Steps: a. Choose an algorithm (e.g., Token Bucket) b. Set up Redis for storing counters/tokens c. Implement Rate Limiter Service d. Integrate with API Gateway

API Gateway: Intercepts incoming requests, Calls Rate Limiter Service



*** Idempotent POST API ***

Designing an idempotent POST API is an important consideration in RESTful design, especially when dealing with scenarios where requests might be repeated due to network issues or client retries. Here's a comprehensive approach to designing an idempotent POST API:

Understand Idempotency:

An operation is idempotent if the result of performing it once is exactly the same as performing it multiple times.
For POST requests, which typically create resources, achieving idempotency requires careful design. (GET etc is already idempotent)
Key Strategies:

a. Use Client-Generated IDs:

Have the client generate and send a unique ID with each request.
Server checks if a resource with this ID already exists before creating a new one.

b. Use Conditional POST:

Similar to conditional GET, but for POST requests.
Use headers like If-None-Match to check if the resource already exists.

c. Implement Idempotency Keys:

Client sends an idempotency key with each request.
Server ensures that requests with the same key are processed only once


*** Designing a highly available system ****


Key Principles: a. Eliminate Single Points of Failure (SPOF) b. Implement Redundancy c. Ensure Fault Isolation d. Enable Fast Recovery e. Design for Scalability

Architecture Components:

a. Load Balancers:

Distribute traffic across multiple servers
Perform health checks and remove unhealthy instances
b. Redundant Servers:

Deploy multiple instances of application servers
Use auto-scaling groups to maintain capacity
c. Distributed Databases:

Implement database replication (Master-Slave or Multi-Master)
Use sharding for horizontal scalability
d. Caching Layer:

Implement distributed caching (e.g., Redis, Memcached)
Reduce load on primary data stores
e. Content Delivery Network (CDN):

Distribute static content globally
Reduce latency and improve availability for static assets
f. Message Queues:

Decouple components using message queues (e.g., RabbitMQ, Kafka)
Enable asynchronous processing
g. Service Discovery:

Implement service registry and discovery (e.g., Consul, Etcd)
Enable dynamic routing and load balancing
Strategies for High Availability:

a. Geographic Distribution:

Deploy across multiple regions/availability zones
Implement global load balancing (e.g., DNS-based routing)
b. Data Replication:

Synchronous replication for critical data
Asynchronous replication for less critical data
c. Failover Mechanisms:

Implement automatic failover for databases and services
Use leader election in distributed systems
d. Circuit Breakers:

Prevent cascading failures by failing fast
Implement retry mechanisms with exponential backoff
e. Monitoring and Alerting:

Implement comprehensive monitoring (e.g., Prometheus, Grafana)
Set up alerts for critical metrics and thresholds
f. Disaster Recovery Plan:

Regular backups with quick restore capabilities
Implement and test disaster recovery procedures
